  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Hot, dry, and smoky conditions continue through this week. \n\n- Isolated thunderstorms remain a threat for southwest Montana\n  including Butte. \n\n- Even hotter, drier, and windy conditions to end the week. \n\n- Cool down to near average temperatures next weekend, though it\n  remains dry. \n\nThe Northern Rockies will remain on the western perifery of a\nbroad ridge of high pressure. Towards the end of this work week,\nthat ridge will strengthen in response to a deep and stationary\nPacific trough beginning to move east into British Colombia. \nBeginning as early as Friday, waves of energy could break off the \ntrough and begin to interact with the ridge. Current modeling has \nridge breakdown late Saturday into early Sunday morning. \n\nSo what can be expected this week? A continuation of hot, dry, and\nsmokey conditions. As valleys stabilize from increasing smoke,\nafternoon breezes will weaken but counter intuitively \ntemperatures trends could cool a degree or 3 due to blocking of \nafternoon solar energy, IE afternoon heating.\n\nIn the near term the main impact will be worsening air quality due\nto smoke. Limit your outdoor activity and please check on those in\nthe community that are sensitive to poor air quality. \n\nThe models could continue to delay or potentially speed up the \nevolution of ridge breakdown. Please continue to follow future \nforecasts as the critical period during ridge breakdown will \nheighten fire weather concerns, due to an increase in winds. \n\nUnfortunately, the single component that the models highly agree\nupon is the lack of moisture or widespread precipitation in the\nnext week.",
